## Test the application

The created project includes a typed application test and a separate test
compiler configuration. `pnpm test` clears its disposable output, compiles the
application and test with stock TypeScript, then executes Node's built-in test
runner against Fadeno's production renderer and the application's raw
stylesheet handler.

<!-- fadeno:include {"path":"examples/v1-app/scenarios/project-creation/expected/app/test/application.test.tsx","language":"tsx"} -->

The executable success output is normalized only for durations:

<!-- fadeno:include {"path":"examples/v1-app/scenarios/application-test/expected/success.txt","language":"text"} -->

The dedicated failure scenario changes one expected heading, observes the
platform assertion, retains normalized human and TAP diagnostics, restores the
source, and proves the stale diagnostic and a seeded stale emitted file both
disappear.

<!-- fadeno:include {"path":"examples/v1-app/scenarios/application-test/expected/diagnostic-human.txt","language":"text"} -->

Disposable test output is also refused if a broadened production TypeScript
configuration tries to include it:

<!-- fadeno:include {"path":"examples/v1-app/scenarios/application-test/expected/build-input-refusal.txt","language":"text"} -->

Run `pnpm check:a0-test` to create and install a project from the current packed
framework and execute the success, failure, correction, flow, and recovery
sequence. The workflow adds no `fadeno test` command, framework test runtime,
public test helper, or private import.

The canonical example package remains the complete executable feature corpus.
`pnpm check` analyzes
framework semantics without writing build output, `pnpm build` runs the
transactional production build, `pnpm dev` starts the retained loopback
development server on its explicit port, and `pnpm start` starts an accepted
production build.

